WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.100 --> 00:00:03.920
Carved into a sandstone cliff on the edge

00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:07.740
of a bygone river,  a hunter draws his bow for the kill.

00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:10.040
He is accompanied by thirteen dogs,

00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:13.100
each with their own markings, two with lines running

00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:15.659
from their necks to the man’s waist.

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:18.340
A new study reveals these engravings may date

00:00:18.340 --> 00:00:21.660
back more than 9,000 years, making them the

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:25.600
earliest depictions of dogs in the archaeological record.

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:28.560
The carvings are from northwestern Saudi Arabia

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:30.700
where archaeologists have catalogued more

00:00:30.700 --> 00:00:33.120
than 1400 rock art panels

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:36.760
containing nearly 7000 animals and humans

00:00:36.760 --> 00:00:39.360
Settlers began entering this part of Saudi Arabia

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:41.620
about 10,000 years ago.

00:00:41.620 --> 00:00:43.080
Early engravings of women,

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:46.000
switched to scenes of cattle, sheep and goats

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:48.740
when the region’s hunter-gatherers became herders

00:00:48.740 --> 00:00:51.900
around 7000 to 8000 years ago.

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:54.960
In between these eras are the early hunting dogs -

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:57.360
medium-sized, with pricked up ears,

00:00:57.360 --> 00:00:59.580
short snouts, and curled tails—

00:00:59.580 --> 00:01:02.720
hallmarks of domestic canines.

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:05.360
All of the dogs look a lot like the Canaan
dog,

00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:07.760
a largely feral breed that has called

00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:09.960
the desert home for thousands of years,

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:12.360
an indication that these ancient people were

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:16.100
breeding dogs adapted for hunting in the desert.

00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:18.140
Researchers found one aspect of these carvings

00:01:18.140 --> 00:01:21.560
to be especially striking - the leashes.

00:01:21.560 --> 00:01:24.300
This would have been early days for any type of dog,

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:26.180
as the animal may have been first

00:01:26.180 --> 00:01:30.200
domesticated as recently as 15,000 years ago

00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:33.400
Until now, the earliest evidence for such restraints

00:01:33.400 --> 00:01:35.540
came from a wall painting in Egypt

00:01:35.540 --> 00:01:38.770
dated to about 5500 years ago

00:01:38.770 --> 00:01:41.380
Arabian hunters may have used the leashes

00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:44.420
to keep valuable dogs close and protected,

00:01:44.420 --> 00:01:47.840
or they could have been a way to train new dogs.

00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:49.980
Or the lines might not be leashes at all,

00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:52.220
but instead be just a symbolic depiction of

00:01:52.220 --> 00:01:54.300
the bond between humans and dogs

00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:55.780
and there is still some doubt

00:01:55.780 --> 00:01:58.180
about the dating of engravings.

00:01:58.180 --> 00:01:59.700
It’s possible that the engravings were

00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:02.000
made more recently, and that the artists were just

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:05.030
representing scenes from their cultural past.

00:02:05.030 --> 00:02:07.540
To confirm the timing, scientists will need

00:02:07.540 --> 00:02:10.360
to link the images to a well-dated archaeological

00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:12.900
site, a difficult task in a region

00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:16.080
with a spotty archaeological record.

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:18.420
If these engravings do indeed prove to be

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:19.800
as old as they seem,

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:21.940
they would offer a fascinating insight

00:02:21.940 --> 00:02:24.080
into how these ancient hunter-gatherers

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:26.500
used the bond between man and dog

00:02:26.500 --> 00:02:29.280
to brave the harsh climate of the Arabian Desert.

